Here is why they mattered. 0x4050 in the Mesa driver was fitted on ten vendor
models at oc = 16, 32, ... 160. The two candidate readings,

    DIV_ROUND_UP(oc,16) parity        and        oc mod 32

give the same answer at every multiple of sixteen, which is every model the rule
was ever fitted on. Until your generator there was no way to build a counter
example, and the parity form was carried on the strength of one shape, oc = 56.

RK3576, my fork of Mesa, your scorer, ROCKET_SEED=7:

    oc      bit exact   within 1   maxdiff   const ref   pinned
    32        28/32       32/32       1          0          0
    48        40/48       48/48       1          0          0
    64        55/64       64/64       1          0          0
    20        18/20       20/20       1          0          0
    40        34/40       40/40       1          0          0
    50        43/50       50/50       1          0          0
    60        53/60       60/60       1          0          0
    90        78/90       90/90       1          0          0
    100       86/100     100/100      1          0          0
    114       98/114     114/114      1          0          0

The last seven are the ones where the two readings disagree, and 40 and 100
disagree the other way round from 20, 50, 60, 90 and 114, which I only noticed
after generating five that all leaned the same way. The driver emits the parity
form. If the modulo form were the truth it would be emitting the wrong constant
at all seven and they would fail. They do not, so the parity form is confirmed
at seven shapes instead of one.

And oc = 48 comes back 40 bit exact, 48 of 48 within 1, which is your RK3588
number to the channel. Different SoC, different Mesa, same arithmetic.

Both of your guards read zero on every line, so none of that is a constant
channel matching itself or a clamp being measured instead of the arithmetic. I
had arrived at the same two rules the hard way, on an op28 disagreement that
turned out to be the reference being clamped, so it was a good feeling to open
your scorer and find them already in it.

I still owe you the oc = 64 polarity answer from the other thread and I have not
forgotten it.

